On 2008-12-09, JG Miller <jimmiller@ymail.com> wrote:
> Please, no retorts of "do it yourself then!", because I'll ignore them
> as usual.  The argument is invalid.  I'm not a developer, but there is
> obviously much development talent and will in the community, and it's
> not being focused to move the game forward with actual releases. There
> is too much coziness with branchitis, and with the philosophy that a
> good product can only be developed by a small, secretive team that
> publishes work at a snail's pace.  It doesn't have to be that way.

Your overall arguments are not without merit --- you're hardly the only
person unhappy with the state of NetHack development --- but "do it
yourself then!" is still valid in some sense. You may not be a developer,
but you either care enough to think about these issues and then write
about them here in an attempt to galvanize someone into action, or
you are just demanding that people do work for your benefit. I'm going
to assume the future... why not pick up an organizational role? Grab
thenewnethack.org or a sourceforge page or whatever, make a feature
list, pick some patches you want to integrate, and try to get people
with development experience on your side. Then make the new NetHack,
if you think it's the thing to do.

I'm not just directing this at you --- if there's will in the
community, as you suggest, it could be someone else who steps up and
does this. The closest we've seen recently is SporkHack, and that's
gotten a mixed reception on RGRN. Personally, I think it's pretty good,
so to get a better reception here, I expect something would have to be
even bigger/better with a good chunk of time behind it. But it's not
undoable, and the fact that no one has stepped up to do it means one of
a few things to me:

 * There isn't actually this "talent and will" you are talking about,
   and people are forced to wait because the DevTeam are the people with
   the talent and will to make NetHack.
 * The talent and will exist, but the people who have it are willing to
   wait, and might be willing to wait another five years, or might not.
 * The talent and will exist, and are currently being tapped by the
   DevTeam, who feel no need to inform you of this fact. (This is what
   I'm hoping for, personally.)
 * The talent and will exist, and the people who have them went off and
   worked on or with other roguelikes instead. (I do think this has
   happened to a certain extent. I know it's where I went.)

There's room for non-coders on development teams of roguelike games
(though I'm pretty sure you've burned your chances on this particular game
with this sort of RGRN post :P ). Crawl has one who does a lot of good
work for the project, and has had others in the past. It's likely that the
NetHack DevTeam does too, though I have no insight into their process.
The lack of development ability doesn't mean there's nothing you, JG,
or you, silent reader, could do toward building or updating a roguelike
game. If you have ideas, or patches you think play well together that
other people might have tried, or organizational resources to offer to
people interested in doing this kind of work, offer them. 

If you really have nothing to offer except screed... well, meh. I like
to give people the benefit of the doubt, though, so I look forward to
seeing what you do to make this happen. If you're looking for places to
start, I always thought the GruntHack codebase had some interesting
features in it that neither Slash'EM nor SporkHack ever picked up, and 
there's a wonderful database of patches over at bilious.homelinux.org. 
Contacting the authors of patches you like directly with offers of
encouragement and resources might be better than posting to a newsgroup
they may only read sporadically.

Best of luck,

-r.


